    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Viral tools are uniquely positioned to isolate and study neural circuits since their intrinsic properties bridge systems and molecular neuroscience. Here, we present two applications of viral tools that reveal novel aspects of the nigrostriatal pathway and the claustrocingulate pathway. In the first project, we examined heterogeneity in the dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) that project to the dorsal striatum. We used TRIO (tracing the relationship between inputs and outputs), a multi-virus monosynaptic tracing approach, to demonstrate that SNc neurons that project to the dorsal medial striatum and dorsal lateral striatum preferentially receive reciprocal inputs from their defined projection region in the dorsal striatum. These two nigrostriatal dopamine neuron subpopulations subsequently were described to have distinct biophysical properties and activity patterns during free behavior. In the second project, we examined the claustrum (CLA), a small brain region found between the cortex and the striatum that forms reciprocal connections with almost all cortical areas. The thin, irregular structure of the CLA has made this region difficult to study to the exclusion of the surrounding cortical structures. Using a brain-wide computational search we found that the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) receives strong inputs from the CLA, but not from surrounding regions. By taking advantage of this connectivity, we used anterograde and retrograde viral tracing techniques to specifically express genetically encoded fluorophores in cortically-projecting claustrum neurons, and claustrum-projecting cingulate neurons. By combining anatomical tracing with the tissue clearing technique CLARITY, we produced a brain-wide visualization of the reciprocal and cross- hemispheric connectivity of the claustrocingulate pathway. Using optogenetics, we stimulated ACC-projecting CLA neurons in awake, behaving mice, revealing an anxiogenic effect. This anatomical characterization and determination of a causal, behaviorally relevant role of the claustrocingulate pathway is an important and enabling step forward in understanding the function of the claustrum.",
